The Weibo leaker known as "<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/instant-digital/">Instant Digital</a>" today <a href="https://weibo.com/5143897135/5276009278738851">said</a> that Samsung Display is set to begin mass production of OLED panels for the first foldable iPhone in May 2026. Meanwhile, the user "<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/fixed-focus-digital/">Fixed Focus Digital</a>" <a href="https://weibo.com/5821279480/5275374771765743">claims</a> that the foldable iPhone's screen may be flatter than many of the existing foldable devices currently on sale, suggesting that Apple has largely <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/11/24/iphone-fold-2026-engineering-validation/">solved the crease problem</a>.<br/>
<br/>
Previous reports have variously described the Samsung panel as as being "<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/07/15/foldable-iphone-crease-free-samsung-display/">virtually crease free</a>" and having "<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/01/12/samsung-showed-off-foldable-iphone-display/">no crease at all</a>." <em>Bloomberg</em>'s <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/mark-gurman/">Mark Gurman</a> claims that the new display technology Apple is using reduces the crease without eliminating it entirely, but the result is "<a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-03-11/apple-s-foldable-iphone-to-feature-ipad-like-interface-when-opened">not perfect</a>."<br/>
<br/>
